  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a wood-like porous material can be selectively modified for three different applications by changing its internal channels and surface chemistry. In each case, a cube with aligned tubular pores is transformed, and the zoomed sketches on the right illustrate the resulting transport pathways.\n\nFor energy storage, carbonization produces a conductive porous carbon with separated electron and ion pathways, while adding a carbon/nanoparticle coating creates more integrated “tri-pathways” for electrons, ions, and gas. This highlights improved multifunctional transport inside the aligned pores.\n\nFor environmental remediation, coating the pore walls with metallic nanoparticles appears to enable filtration, with wastewater entering from above and clean water exiting through the channels. A different treatment, surface carbonization, is shown driving solar- or heat-assisted evaporation, where seawater or wastewater rises through the structure and vapour escapes from the darkened top layer.\n\nFor ionic nanofluidics, delignification opens well-defined vertical nanochannels that support fast ion transport, and combining delignification with crosslinking seems to stabilize these channels while maintaining rapid ion movement. Overall, the figure emphasizes a common porous scaffold engineered into different functional transport architectures for storage, purification, and ion conduction.",
